{
  "result": "At dusk I like to wander into the city's oldest library, a Romanesque shell of carved limestone whose windows flicker like embered honey. The automatic doors hiss, surrendering me to silence that isn\u2019t silent at all: it\u2019s alive with the breath of climate vents, the distant crinkle of pages, and the faint throat-clearing of people respectfully pretending not to exist. I drift past genealogy stations and public-policy tomes until I reach the fiction stacks, where spines lean together like conspirators sharing dusty gossip. Here, time loosens its belt. Characters dream in suspended animation; metaphors wait, claws sheathed, for curious fingers. I slide out a battered copy of Calvino and feel an electric pulse, as if the book recognizes a companionable soul. When I open it, the scent of vanillin and inked fiber rises, a perfume stronger than any designer\u2019s ambition. Outside, traffic braids itself into impatient knots, but inside, sentences unspool calm ribbons across my mind. The world shrinks to the width of margin notes left by strangers: underlined aphorisms, an arguing question mark, a heart drawn after the word \u201coblivion.\u201d I wonder about the annotator\u2014were they soothed, heartbroken, caffeinated beyond reason? Their ghostly commentary reminds me that reading is a duet performed across decades, a handshake where only one palm is warm. Eventually the lights blink, announcing closing time. I reshelve Calvino, whisper thanks, and step back into the purple evening, carrying an invisible library stitched behind my eyelids. Its stories will glow tomorrow, waiting, like lanterns underwater, quietly.",
